I'm no emacs expert, but I have some vague memories from my student
days before I converted to vi which might help you:

Rule #1: when in doubt, press C-g C-g C-g.  Three is usually overkill,
but ought to be enough to get you out of anything waiting unexpectedly
for input you've managed to get yourself into.

C-x k closes a buffer (<Enter> for the current buffer); that should
get you out of the tutorial if you seem stuck.

Try running xemacs instead of emacs.  That should give you some
limited mouse control until you can wean yourself away from it.  (This
will only work if you have X up and running in your cygwin
environment)

